r=add(2,'Candied Pears with Coconut Filling and Almond Custard',4,3000,'Pears absorb syrup over three days, then are filled with coconut paste and browned under a thick almond-custard coating.',[I('pears',600,'g','small firm pears, weighed after peeling and coring, about four','Use firm small pears that hold their shape; soft ripe pears are unsuitable for repeated heating.'),S(600),W(600),I('coconut',50,'g','unsweetened desiccated coconut','Use 50 g finely ground almonds for a different filling, noting almonds are also in the coating.'),S(30,'fillsugar'),W(50,'fillwater'),I('almonds',100,'g','finely ground blanched almonds','Use 100 g finely ground sunflower seeds for a different flavor and color.'),I('reserved',100,'ml','pear cooking syrup reserved from this recipe','Use the syrup produced in the earlier steps.')]+custard_items(),['Day 1: Prepare {{pears}}, leaving the fruit whole with a cavity for filling. Dissolve {{sugar}} in {{water}} in a snug saucepan. Add the pears and simmer very gently for 20–35 minutes, turning carefully, until just tender but intact.','Transfer pears and syrup to shallow heatproof containers. Cool briefly and refrigerate within 2 hours at 4°C (40°F) or colder. Leave refrigerated overnight; never hold the fruit overnight at room temperature.','Day 2: Return fruit and syrup to the pan, heat gently to a simmer and cook for 10 minutes. Cool and refrigerate again as on day 1.','Day 3: Gently simmer fruit and syrup for another 10 minutes. Lift the pears onto a rack over a tray to drain. Measure {{reserved}} for the coating; cool and refrigerate the unused syrup separately.','Cook {{coconut}}, {{fillsugar}} and {{fillwater}} in a small pan for 3–5 minutes, stirring until a thick moist paste forms. Let cool enough to handle and spoon into the pear cavities.']+custard_steps()+['Stir {{almonds}} and the reserved syrup into the warm custard. Cook gently, stirring for 2–3 minutes, until thick enough to coat the pears; do not boil.','Heat the oven grill/broiler. Place the filled pears in a metal baking dish, coat with the almond custard and brown briefly 15–20 cm below the heat, about 2–4 minutes, watching constantly. Serve warm.'],'The shorter duplicate transcription is completed from the fuller same-book recipe. Equal prepared fruit/sugar weights and three-day absorption are retained; shorter controlled reheats replace repeated multi-hour boils. Coconut paste, custard and coating quantities are supplied. Bricks, stones and the historical overhead brazier are equipment, not ingredients; a modern broiler replaces them. Total time includes two overnight refrigerator holds.',refs=[EGG])
r=add(6,'Perones Encantullados (Candied Apples with Spices)',4,3000,'Firm apples absorb sugar syrup over three days and finish with clove and cinnamon, without the filled pear recipe’s stuffing.',[I('apples',600,'g','firm perón apples, peeled and cored, about four','Use another firm tart cooking apple; avoid varieties that collapse quickly.'),S(600),W(600),clove(8),I('cinnamon',3,'','small cinnamon sticks, broken into short pieces','Use 0.75 tsp ground cinnamon, leaving it in the syrup.')],['Dissolve {{sugar}} in {{water}} in a snug saucepan. Add {{apples}} and simmer very gently for 15–25 minutes until just tender but intact, turning carefully.','Transfer fruit and syrup to shallow containers, cool briefly and refrigerate within 2 hours at 4°C (40°F) or colder. Leave overnight.','On the next day, heat fruit and syrup gently to a simmer and cook for 10 minutes. Cool and refrigerate overnight again.','On day 3, add {{cloves}} and {{cinnamon}} to the syrup and simmer gently for 10 minutes. Lift out the apples, remove all whole spices and reduce the syrup for 5–10 minutes if needed. Spoon over the fruit and serve.'],'Perón is identified by Mexican agricultural sources as an apple variety, correcting the English extraction’s pear label. The missing candied-fruit cross-reference is expanded. Spices infuse the syrup and are removed rather than being left as hard garnish. This modern home adaptation retains three-day absorption but is not a shelf-stable confection.',refs=[PERON])
r=add(7,'Perones Fritos (Battered Apple Rings)',4,35,'Thin apple rings are coated in an egg-yolk batter and fried until crisp for a savory garnish.',[I('apples',3,'','firm perón apples','Use three firm tart cooking apples of similar size.'),I('flour',100,'g','all-purpose flour','Use 100 g a gluten-free batter blend; texture may differ.'),yolk(2),I('oil',15,'ml','neutral vegetable oil','Use the same volume of mild olive oil.'),I('salt',1,'g','fine salt','Reduce or omit.'),W(120),I('fryingfat',500,'g','food-grade lard for frying','Use 550 ml neutral high-heat vegetable oil; do not use butter.')],['Rinse {{apples}}, remove the cores and cut into 5 mm rings. Pat dry.','Whisk {{flour}}, {{yolks}}, {{oil}}, {{salt}} and {{water}} into a smooth batter that coats a ring lightly. Let stand for 5 minutes.','Heat {{fryingfat}} in a heavy deep pan to 175°C (347°F), using enough for about 3 cm depth but filling the pan no more than one-third.','Dip rings in batter and fry in small batches for 2–3 minutes per side until golden and the apples are tender. Maintain the frying temperature between batches. Drain on a rack and serve hot.'],'Perones are adapted as apples, correcting the extracted pear translation. Two yolks are selected from the source range. Frying fat is a working quantity, largely left in the pan, not a per-serving consumed amount. Batter and frying details are supplied.',refs=[PERON])
