r=add(8,'Coelho Ensopado com Gerumbeba (Rabbit with Cactus Pads)',4,1560,'Rabbit marinates with tomato, garlic and pepper, then gently braises with tender edible cactus pads.',[I('rabbit',1200,'g','jointed farm-raised rabbit, bone-in','Use 1200 g bone-in chicken thighs for a different dish; cook chicken to 74°C (165°F).'),I('salt',5,'g','fine salt','Reduce, particularly if using salted stock.'),I('garlic',12,'g','peeled garlic, crushed','Use 6 g garlic paste without added salt.'),I('pepper',.5,'tsp','ground black pepper','Reduce or omit.'),I('tomatoes',300,'g','ripe tomatoes, chopped','Use 300 g canned chopped tomatoes.'),I('fat',40,'g','food-grade lard','Use 40 g neutral vegetable oil.'),nopales(300),I('flour',8,'g','all-purpose flour','Use 8 g a gluten-free flour blend suited to sauces.'),I('stock',500,'ml','unsalted chicken stock','Use 500 ml unsalted vegetable stock or water.')],['Mix {{rabbit}} with {{salt}}, {{garlic}}, {{pepper}} and {{tomatoes}} in a covered nonreactive container. Refrigerate at 4°C (40°F) or colder for 24 hours.','Lift out the rabbit, retaining the marinade. Heat {{fat}} in a heavy pot and brown the rabbit in batches. Add all the reserved marinade and cook, stirring, for 5 minutes.','Cut {{nopales}} into 2 cm pieces, add to the pot and stir in {{flour}}. Cook for 2 minutes, then stir in {{stock}}.','Bring to a simmer, cover and cook gently for 60–90 minutes until the rabbit is tender and reaches at least 71°C (160°F) in the thickest meat, away from bone. Add a little water if needed to prevent drying. Remove any loose bones before serving.'],'Gerumbeba is interpreted as edible cactus pads in this cookbook, correcting the fruit translation. Commercially cleaned pads replace spine-burning preparation. The missing braise and refrigerator handling are supplied; timing includes the source 24-hour marinade.','Main dishes',[NOPAL,'https://www.foodsafety.gov/food-safety-charts/safe-minimum-internal-temperatures'])
r=add(9,'Gerumbeba Guisada (Cactus Pads with Citrus and Yolk)',4,35,'Tender cactus-pad pieces are lightly thickened and finished with parsley and a sour-orange egg-yolk liaison.',[nopales(500),I('flour',15,'g','all-purpose flour','Use 15 g a gluten-free flour blend suited to sauces.'),I('fat',25,'g','food-grade lard','Use 25 g neutral vegetable oil.'),W(240),I('salt',2,'g','fine salt','Reduce or omit.'),I('pepper',.25,'tsp','ground black pepper','Omit for a milder dish.'),I('parsley',5,'g','fresh parsley, finely chopped','Use 5 g cilantro for a different herbal flavor.'),yolk(1),I('orange',30,'ml','sour orange juice','Use 15 ml orange juice mixed with 15 ml lime juice.')],['Cut {{nopales}} into 2 cm pieces and pat dry. Toss with {{flour}}. Heat {{fat}} in a saucepan and sauté the coated pieces for 3–4 minutes, stirring.','Add {{water}}, {{salt}}, {{pepper}} and {{parsley}}. Simmer gently for 15 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the pads are tender and the liquid lightly thickened.','Whisk {{yolks}} with {{orange}} in a bowl. Gradually whisk in several spoonfuls of hot sauce, then return to the pan.','Stir over low heat until the sauce reaches 71°C (160°F), without boiling. Serve immediately.'],'The mistranslated fruit is corrected to edible cactus pads. Fully de-spined commercial pads replace open-flame spine removal. All missing quantities and controlled egg heating are supplied.','Side dishes',[NOPAL,EGG])
r=add(31,'Two-Texture Pear Compote',4,75,'Whole pears are served beneath a lemon syrup thickened with a second portion of cooked pear pieces.',[P(7),S(454),W(240),L(30)],['Peel {{pears}}. Keep four of every seven pears whole and scoop out their cores from the base; core and chop the remaining three. When scaling, keep the same approximate whole-to-chopped proportion.','Heat {{sugar}}, {{water}} and {{lemon}} in a snug saucepan until dissolved. Add the chopped pears and simmer for 10 minutes.','Add the whole pears and simmer gently, covered, for 25–45 minutes until tender, turning and basting carefully. Add small splashes of water if needed. Lift the whole pears onto a dish.','Mash the pear pieces into their syrup, or press through a sieve. Simmer for a few minutes until lightly thickened, then spoon over the whole pears and serve.'],'Seven source pears are interpreted as four whole and three for the coating, resolving the inconsistent instruction to chop four or five and still use whole pears. Starting water is increased to 240 ml from the conflicting quarter/half-cup amounts. Yield is four whole-pear portions; these choices are modern adaptations.')
r=add(36,'Sautéed Pears with Chilled Chocolate-Caramel Sauce',4,125,'Browned pear quarters are paired with a chilled chocolate sauce enriched with cream and a butter-caramel finish.',[P(4),B(20),I('chocolate',57,'g','sweet eating chocolate, chopped','Use 57 g semisweet chocolate for a darker, less sweet sauce.'),S(13),milk(300),I('arrowroot',3,'g','arrowroot starch','Use 3 g cornstarch; simmer for 1 minute to cook it.'),cream(60),I('salt',.2,'g','fine salt','Omit if using salted butter.'),B(21,'caramelbutter'),S(30,'caramelsugar'),I('vanilla',2.5,'ml','vanilla extract','Use 1 g vanilla paste, or omit.')],['Combine {{chocolate}}, {{sugar}} and {{milk}} in a heatproof bowl over barely simmering water, keeping the bowl above the water. Stir until melted and smooth, about 5 minutes.','Mix {{arrowroot}} with {{cream}} and whisk into the warm chocolate mixture with {{salt}}. Continue heating and stirring for 5–10 minutes until lightly thickened.','In a small light-colored saucepan, heat {{caramelbutter}} with {{caramelsugar}} over medium-low heat, stirring, until the sugar dissolves and the mixture becomes golden amber. Remove from the heat immediately.','Carefully whisk the warm chocolate mixture into the caramel a little at a time; it may bubble. Stir in {{vanilla}}, pour into a shallow container, cool briefly and refrigerate for at least 1 hour.','Just before serving, peel {{pears}}, quarter and remove cores. Melt {{butter}} in a skillet and sauté the quarters for 8–12 minutes, turning, until golden and tender. Serve with the chilled sauce.'],'Separate cooking and caramel butter quantities are supplied. Modern 300 ml milk and 60 ml cream replace source cups. Granulated sugar replaces powdered sugar in the caramel for more predictable browning; the chocolate, arrowroot and chilled-sauce character remain.')
